Troubleshooting Tips
Issue: Cannot find log files

Solution: Check if logging_collector is enabled in postgresql.conf
Solution: Verify PostgreSQL is running: sudo systemctl status postgresql
Issue: Log file is empty

Solution: Ensure you generated database activity after enabling logging
Solution: Check log_statement setting in postgresql.conf
Issue: Permission denied when accessing logs

Solution: Use sudo or copy logs to your home directory with proper ownership
Issue: grep commands return no results

Solution: Verify the log file contains data: wc -l ~/db_log.txt
Solution: Check if you're searching the correct log file
Conclusion
In this lab, you learned how to:

Locate PostgreSQL log files in the Linux filesystem
Configure PostgreSQL to generate detailed logs
Use grep and shell scripts to analyze log contents
Identify slow queries that may need optimization
Detect and categorize database errors
Create documentation and reports from log analysis
Why This Matters: Database logs are essential for troubleshooting performance issues, identifying security problems, and understanding application behavior. Regular log review helps maintain healthy database systems and prevents small issues from becoming major problems.

